<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macau is a territory on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a>. The Portuguese established a settlement there in the sixteenth century, making it an important hub for trade between China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It remained under Portuguese administration until 1999, making it one of the last remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macau.jpg" alt="Macau" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see File history · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Macau.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Macau lies on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s southern coast, 60 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, on the south shore of Zhongshan Island on the western side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> estuary. It consists of the Macau Peninsula, Taipa, Coloane and the Cotai reclamation zone between them. The peninsula was originally a separate island with hilly terrain that became a tombolo as a sandbar formed, and natural sedimentation and land reclamation have enlarged it. The land area grew from 10.28 square kilometres in the late 19th century to 32.9 square kilometres in 2018.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> (221–206 BCE) the region came under Panyu County, Nanhai Commandery, and it is first known to have been settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. In 1152, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, it fell under the new Xiangshan County. In 1277 about 50,000 refugees fleeing the Mongol conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> settled on the coast. The earliest written record of the name, A Ma Gang, is in a letter of 20 November 1555. It refers to the sea goddess Matsu, or A-Ma, whose temple the local people took the Portuguese to be asking about when they gave the name Ma Kok.</p><p>The explorer Jorge Álvares, the first European to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by sea, arrived in 1513. Portuguese merchants set up a trading post at Tamão, now Tuen Mun, but after their expulsion in 1521 the Ming and Portuguese navies clashed. Portugal lost two ships at the Battle of Tamão in 1521 and two more at the Battle of Sincouwaan; later defeats came at Shuangyu in 1548 and near Dongshan County in 1549. Merchants kept trying to settle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> estuary and finally settled at Macau.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> first leased the territory to Portugal as a trading post in 1557. Portugal paid an annual rent and administered it under Chinese sovereignty until 1887, when the Sino-Portuguese Treaty of Peking gave it perpetual colonial rights. Portuguese rule lasted until the handover to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1999. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-portuguese-joint-declaration">Sino-Portuguese Joint Declaration</a> provided for economic and administrative continuity, and the Basic Law of Macao, under the principle of one country, two systems, serves as the regional constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Macau is a special administrative region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with a population of about 720,000 and one of the highest densities in the world. Its economy rests largely on casino gaming and tourism. More than four centuries of Chinese and Portuguese contact have left a mixed culture, with Macanese people of mixed Portuguese ancestry, and a downtown that blends Portuguese, French, English, Italian and Chinese architecture. The historic centre was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2005.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Portuguese Empire that grew from these voyages lasted until 1999, when <b>Macau</b> was handed to China, and left Portuguese as the language of Brazil, Angola, Mozambique and other countries, today spoken by more than 250 million people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hong Kong returned from British rule on 1 July 1997 and <b>Macau</b> from Portuguese rule on 20 December 1999, both as special administrative regions under the formula of ‘one country, two systems’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese language became, and remains, a major world language with more than 250 million speakers, used officially in Brazil, Angola, Mozambique, Cape Verde, Guinea-Bissau, São Tomé and Príncipe, East Timor and <b>Macau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The characters themselves continued to evolve: the People’s Republic of China introduced simplified characters in the 1950s and 1960s, while Taiwan, Hong Kong and <b>Macau</b> kept the traditional forms.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Hong Kong and <b>Macau</b>, special administrative regions of China, are often described as city-states without sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Portugal built a chain of fortified trading posts from Goa to <b>Macau</b> and plantations in Brazil, and in the 17th century the Dutch, English and French founded colonies in North America, the Caribbean and Asia.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
